Subject: Run compiled functions that contain instructions only the monolith runs on a record too

What this does
  - The record path of `JS_CallInternal` (patches 0339 and 0340) no longer requires the
    interpreter's own record eligibility (every instruction has a threaded handler). A compiled
    function runs its instructions itself or through the step twin of the monolith's body,
    which exist for every instruction, so the threaded loop is needed only to resume after a
    deopt, and the generated code's one deopt (a pc that is not an instruction start) is
    unreachable. If a frame ever were resumed in a function the threaded loop cannot run, the
    call throws an internal error instead of mis-running it.
  - Why that holds: every exit of the generated code lands in the generic body, which covers
    every pc, so the threaded loop is never asked to run such a function. A rate-based
    switch-off goes to the generic body as well, and an unbound function (`js_aot_unbind`, or a
    layout mismatch) has `aot_fn == NULL`, so the head does not apply and the normal path runs
    it. A model whose exits resume the interpreter must resume monolith-only functions through
    a path that can run them, so the internal error is unreachable in either model.

Why we need it
  `check_brand`, `check_ctor_return`, `set_home_object`, `define_method`, `typeof` and a few
  more are monolith-only, so any function containing one (private methods, derived
  constructors, class bodies) kept the full activation however simple it was: a private-method
  program was 1.09x of the interpreter and is 1.01x.

